WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.745
How big is the moon?

00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:07.823
In the night sky the moon seems rather small,
but in reality it really isn't.

00:00:07.823 --> 00:00:10.312
Central Europe would easily fit under it.

00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:13.592
The surface area of the moon is huge.

00:00:13.597 --> 00:00:20.735
To give you some perspective, it's as big as the whole European
continent, China, the USA, Brazil, and South Africa together.

00:00:21.353 --> 00:00:25.534
Weight-wise, the moon is only about 1/81th of the mass of Earth,

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:28.061
but how big is it in relation to Earth?

00:00:28.358 --> 00:00:33.528
Quite big actually, our moon is the biggest in
the solar system in relation to its planet,

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:39.526
and it's the fifth largest moon in the solar system.
Quite a bit bigger than the dwarf planet Pluto.

00:00:39.884 --> 00:00:46.589
Now if the moon is really that huge,
how far away is it from us to seem this small in the night sky?

00:00:47.169 --> 00:00:55.900
370 km, as far as the ISS maybe? No.
At that distance, gravitational forces would rip the moon apart.

00:00:56.223 --> 00:01:00.964
At 36000 km, it would be as far away as our most distant satellite.

00:01:01.296 --> 00:01:05.865
Let's adjust the distance to reality.
Pretty far away, right?

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:14.093
A 747 would need 28 straight days to fly to the moon,
and even with our current technology, we'd need two full days.

00:01:14.093 --> 00:01:19.219
And in a car, a lot of time.
So how big is the moon?

00:01:20.137 --> 00:01:27.822
Well, it kind of depends how you define "big";
but seriously though, it's pretty big.

00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:36.034
Hey Youtube, we'll try something new this month, Mo(o)n May.
One video about cool moon stuff each Monday in May.

00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:37.523
So, see you next week!

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
